<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Heat hydration principles"><div><strong>Heat raises strain</strong><span>Higher environmental heat can increase cardiovascular and thermoregulatory demands during exercise.</span></div><div><strong>Sweat is individual</strong><span>Rate and sodium concentration vary with athlete, intensity, climate and acclimatization.</span></div><div><strong>Cooling still matters</strong><span>A drink cannot substitute for pacing, shade, airflow, rest or emergency heat-illness response.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Why heat changes the plan</p><h2>Sweat cools the body, but it also moves water and electrolytes out</h2><p>Evaporating sweat is one of the body's main cooling mechanisms during exercise. In hot environments, especially with high work rates, sweat losses can accumulate quickly and make access to fluid more important.</p><p>Recent evidence also shows that heat and dehydration can alter substrate use during prolonged endurance work. These physiological effects do not create one universal prescription, but they reinforce the value of planning before the session starts.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/3771085/pexels-photo-3771085.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Athlete pausing to drink water during warm-weather training" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Sweat sodium</p><h2>Salty sweaters are real, but measurement needs care</h2><p>Sweat sodium concentration differs markedly between people. It can also change with heat acclimatization and testing method, which is why a single local patch measurement should not be treated as a permanent personal number.</p><p>High sweat rate plus high sweat sodium can create a larger sodium loss over a long session. That can make electrolyte replacement more relevant, but it still needs to fit with total fluid intake, food, health conditions and the actual duration of sweating.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Heat strategy</p><h2>Reduce the problem before trying to drink your way out of it</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Acclimatize</h3><p>Repeated heat exposure can improve thermoregulatory responses. Increase exposure progressively rather than jumping directly into full load.</p></article><article><h3>Shift timing</h3><p>When possible, move demanding sessions away from the hottest part of the day.</p></article><article><h3>Plan access</h3><p>Know where water and electrolyte drinks will be available before the session begins.</p></article><article><h3>Watch symptoms</h3><p>Confusion, collapse, severe weakness, loss of coordination or other concerning heat-illness signs require immediate action, not more training.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate in the heat</p><h2>Electrolyte-containing fluid can be useful when sweating is prolonged</h2><p>Best Hydrate provides water, glucose and listed electrolyte sources when prepared as directed. This can make it a practical beverage option during prolonged sweating.</p><p>That does not mean every hot workout needs an electrolyte product. Shorter or lighter sessions may be adequately supported by ordinary water and meals. Longer, hotter or higher-sweat sessions are where individual planning becomes more important.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Safety</span><h3>Heat illness is not a hydration marketing opportunity.</h3><p>Stop activity and follow appropriate emergency guidance when serious heat-illness symptoms appear.</p></aside></section>

